            <ArticleTitle>[Fatal anaphylaxis after eating wild boar meat in a patient with pork-cat syndrome].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>163-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Crossed allergy between pork and cat epithelia was described by us in 1994. It is due to serum albumin. Nowadays, other bio-chemical observations allow &quot;completion&quot; of the syndrome by extension of the crossed reactivity between other mammal meats and other epithelia of dog and horse. The authors report an observation of the pork-cat syndrome (developing in the form of anaphylaxis, and then ending in the death of the patient), following consumption of wild boar meat. Co-factors, such as effort, taking alcohol or hormonal condition may complicate the picture to make diagnosis more difficult.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
